# DTF tier manifest: oci-manifest-scrub
#   (artifact-keeper#4666; shipped in 1.11.1)
# Profile-set: filesystem. The oracle pushes an OCI image over /v2 (config, two
# layers, manifest), runs POST /api/v1/admin/storage-scrub {"repair":false}
# scoped to that repository, then truncates the stored manifest body through
# a helper container that mounts the backend's volume, and scrubs again.
#
# THE BUG (RED on 1.11.0)
#   #4666  Since #3601 an OCI manifest artifact's artifacts.size_bytes holds
#          the IMAGE size (config plus layers); the scrub compared it with the
#          stored manifest body, so every intact manifest was a `corrupt`
#          finding (storage_scrub_findings) and real damage was buried in the
#          noise. Fixed: a manifest is checked against oci_manifests.size_bytes
#          (the body length; digest-only when no record exists yet), and that
#          body length is a finding's expected_size.
#
# THE GATES
#   (CONTROL) every image: the push records body length and image size as
#     different numbers; the scrub walks the repository and counts the blobs
#     intact; a manifest truncated by 20 bytes is reported corrupt.
#   (FIX) intact image -> corrupt=0 and no finding row; the truncated
#     manifest's finding has expected_size == oci_manifests.size_bytes. RED on
#     1.11.0 (corrupt=1 with expected_size = image size on the intact image),
#     GREEN on 1.11.1 and later.
#
# NOT GATED: a manifest with no oci_manifests record yet (digest-only check);
# the S3 backend (storage-integrity-s3 covers the scrub there).
#
# This is the "add an OCI repository to storage-integrity" facet of the audit,
# kept as its own small tier so storage-integrity's case count is unchanged.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
